You are checking visual reference facts against a muted video excerpt and timestamped frames. Inspect the moving image first. Numbered candidates may contain fabricated actions, incorrect objects or technique names, wrong people, broad activity summaries, wrong timing, or overly strong details. Independently check the same person, object, direction, granularity and timing for each ID. For communication require visible articulatory change during this interval; a face, stance, captions or voice cues alone cannot suffice. A title or static view is not a performing person. For a claimed contact, tool insertion, small implement or completed outcome require an actually visible before/contact/after change. For technique names require mechanics sufficient to distinguish plausible alternatives. Use any timeline printed in candidate prose only as a hypothesis.
Return {"checks":[{"id":"exact ID","verdict":"supported|weaken|unclear|contradicted","evidence":"specific visual cue in English","label":"English action phrase","brief":"one English sentence","detail":"visible mechanics in English"}]}. Use supported only for a specific event at this time with a defensible description. Use weaken when the same underlying movement is clear but a descriptive claim must be removed; supply a complete conservative replacement (same actor, interval and event kind). Use unclear when only an activity category can be recognized, when an event boundary is implausible or the alleged small object/relationship cannot be seen. Use contradicted when the scene shows a different action or an empty/static scene. Checking this window cannot establish a cause or unseen event. Do not repeat unverified details just because a prompt contains them.
